---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const fiscalCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'y', label: 'GDP growth rate', min: -4, max: 6, step: 0.5, value: 2, unit: '%' },
    { name: 'i', label: 'average interest rate on debt', min: 0, max: 8, step: 0.5, value: 3, unit: '%' },
    { name: 'd', label: 'debt-to-GDP ratio', min: 20, max: 250, step: 5, value: 100, unit: '%' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'interest bill (% of GDP)', expr: 'd*i/100' },
    { label: 'debt trajectory: rising if interest > growth', expr: 'i - y', unit: '%/yr drift' },
    { label: 'primary surplus needed to stabilise', expr: '(i - y)*d/100', unit: '% of GDP' },
  ],
};

const fiscalQuizCfg = {
  questions: [
    {
      q: 'A government spends £10 billion on infrastructure in a slump, and households spend 80% of any extra income (MPC = 0.8). The total rise in GDP is roughly:',
      choices: [
        '£10 billion',
        'About £50 billion — the multiplier 1/(1 − 0.8) = 5: the £10bn becomes income, 80% is re-spent, and the chain compounds (in practice, leakages to taxes, imports and saving shrink it well below 5)',
        '£8 billion',
        'Zero — spending is just moving money',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'One person\'s spending is another\'s income, so deficit spending circulates: £10bn → £8bn re-spent → £6.4bn → … totalling £50bn in the frictionless case. Real multipliers are smaller (leakages) but larger in deep slumps with idle resources than near full employment — which is why timing, not just size, decides whether stimulus works.',
    },
    {
      q: 'A country has debt of 100% of GDP, an average interest rate of 3%, and nominal GDP growth of 5%. To keep the debt ratio from rising, it needs:',
      choices: [
        'A balanced budget',
        'Nothing — with growth (5%) above the interest rate (3%), the debt ratio drifts down even with a small primary deficit; growth itself does the stabilising',
        'A primary surplus of 3% of GDP',
        'To default',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'The debt-dynamics arithmetic: the ratio rises only when the interest–growth differential (r − g) is positive and not offset by a primary surplus. Post-war UK and the US grew their debts down from over 120% of GDP mostly this way — not through austerity, but through growth above the interest rate.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Why does borrowing in your own currency make a debt crisis less likely?',
      choices: [
        'Because your own currency is stronger',
        'Because a government issuing debt in currency it prints cannot be forced into involuntary default — the worst case is inflation, not bankruptcy; countries borrowing in dollars or (like Greece) sharing a currency they do not print face genuine default risk',
        'Because domestic lenders are kinder',
        'Because exchange rates are fixed',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'The distinction separates Japan (debt over 250% of GDP, borrowing in yen, no crisis) from Argentina and Greece. It is not a licence: the own-currency escape valve is inflation, and losing it is why eurozone members adopted harsher fiscal rules than the US or UK ever accepted.',
    },
    {
      q: 'During a boom, tax receipts rise and unemployment benefit payments fall automatically. This is:',
      choices: [
        'Discretionary fiscal policy',
        'The automatic stabilisers at work: the budget moves counter-cyclically with no new legislation — moderating booms and cushioning slumps, and precisely why deficits widen in recessions even under unchanged policy',
        'Austerity',
        'Evidence of government waste',
      ],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Progressive taxes and unemployment benefits are built-in shock absorbers: they lean against the cycle without waiting for parliaments. Discretionary policy — the legislated stimulus or austerity — is the part politicians argue about; the automatic part is why every recession shows a deficit that is nobody\'s decision.',
    },
  ],
};

const fpStabiliserSort = {
  "prompt": "Automatic stabiliser, or discretionary policy?",
  "buckets": [
    "automatic stabiliser",
    "discretionary policy"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "unemployment benefits rise as jobs disappear",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "parliament passes a new infrastructure programme",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "tax receipts fall when incomes fall",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "the government cuts VAT for one year",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "pension payments rise with the rules",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "a one-off stimulus cheque is legislated",
      "bucket": 1
    }
  ],
  "why": "Automatic stabilisers widen the deficit without a vote; discretionary policy is the part politicians argue about."
};

const fpLeversMatch = {
  "prompt": "Pair each fiscal lever with what it does:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "government purchases",
      "right": "build a bridge — buys output directly"
    },
    {
      "left": "transfer payments",
      "right": "a pension or benefit — redistributes without buying output"
    },
    {
      "left": "taxes",
      "right": "withdraw purchasing power; cutting them is a stimulus"
    }
  ],
  "why": "Purchases enter G directly; transfers stimulate only when recipients spend them; taxes change the purchasing power left in private hands."
};

const fpMultiplierEst = {
  "prompt": "A £10bn programme is spent by households with MPC = 0.8. In the frictionless multiplier, what is the total rise in GDP?",
  "unit": "£bn",
  "answer": 50,
  "factor": 1.1,
  "why": "Multiplier = 1/(1 − 0.8) = 5, so £10bn × 5 = £50bn. Leakages into taxes, imports and saving make the real figure smaller.",
  "context": "with MPC 0.6 the multiplier would be 2.5"
};

const fpDebtPlace = {
  "prompt": "Debt is 100% of GDP, the interest rate is 5% and nominal growth is 3%. What primary surplus, as a share of GDP, stabilises the ratio? Place it.",
  "labels": [
    "0%",
    "1%",
    "2%",
    "3%",
    "4%"
  ],
  "scale": {
    "min": 0,
    "max": 4
  },
  "answer": 2,
  "tolerance": 0.1,
  "why": "(i − y) × D = (5% − 3%) × 100% = 2% of GDP. Without that surplus the ratio drifts up."
};

const fpBurdenJudge = {
  "statements": [
    {
      "s": "If debt is held domestically, servicing it is largely a transfer from future taxpayers to future bondholders.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "The payment circulates within the country; the question becomes distributional rather than an international loss."
    },
    {
      "s": "If the debt is owed to ourselves, it has no economic costs at all.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "Incentive costs from the taxes that service it, the foreign-held share, and lost crisis room all survive the accounting."
    },
    {
      "s": "A heavily indebted country has less room to respond to the next crisis.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "Pre-existing debt constrains the fiscal response, as the 2010 eurozone members discovered."
    },
    {
      "s": "Bond interest flows disproportionately to wealthier savers.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "The servicing is financed by broader taxpayers and paid to bondholders, a quietly regressive transfer."
    }
  ]
};

const fpCurrencyBranch = {
  "root": "Two governments have debt at 150% of GDP. One borrows in its own currency; the other borrows in dollars. Trace the crisis menu.",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "Markets begin to doubt repayment. Which government faces genuine default risk?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "The dollar borrower — it cannot print dollars",
          "next": "right",
          "feedback": "Yes — it must find dollars or restructure."
        },
        {
          "label": "The own-currency borrower — it must default",
          "next": "own",
          "feedback": "It can always create the currency; the risk shifts to inflation."
        },
        {
          "label": "Both face identical risk",
          "next": "same",
          "feedback": "The currency changes the menu completely."
        }
      ]
    },
    "right": {
      "text": "The foreign-currency borrower can be forced into default: yields spike, refinancing closes, and the IMF arrives with conditions. The own-currency government's crisis mode is inflation, not bankruptcy."
    },
    "own": {
      "text": "An own-currency government cannot be forced into involuntary default — it can print. The cost is inflation, which taxes savers.",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Compare the two cases",
          "next": "right",
          "feedback": "Foreign-currency debt risks default; own-currency debt risks inflation."
        }
      ]
    },
    "same": {
      "text": "The currency is the biggest risk factor: own-currency debt risks inflation, foreign-currency debt risks default.",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Which is which?",
          "next": "right",
          "feedback": "The dollar borrower faces the default wall."
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  "why": "Own-currency debt cannot force involuntary default — the escape valve is inflation; foreign-currency and shared-currency debt can force a restructuring."
};

const fpCrisisOrder = {
  "prompt": "A rollover crisis unfolds — put the sequence in order:",
  "items": [
    "Markets begin to doubt the government's ability to repay",
    "New bond issues demand punitive yields",
    "The interest bill eats a growing share of the budget",
    "Refinancing becomes harder and the spiral turns self-fulfilling",
    "A bailout or restructuring follows"
  ],
  "why": "The rollover squeeze is self-reinforcing: doubt raises yields, higher yields worsen the budget, and worse budgets confirm the doubt."
};

const fpDeficitCompare = {
  "prompt": "Two ways to read a deficit — drag the divider:",
  "before": {
    "label": "headline deficit",
    "text": "the actual budget balance this year — widened automatically by recession"
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "structural deficit",
    "text": "the balance after stripping out the cycle — the underlying policy stance"
  },
  "why": "The cyclical part is the stabilisers doing their job; the structural part is the choice. Comparing budgets across years without that distinction misreads the stance."
};

const fpAusteritySpot = {
  "prompt": "Find the line where the austerity arithmetic goes wrong:",
  "lines": [
    "After 2010, eurozone governments cut spending to reassure bond markets.",
    "The IMF assumed multipliers around 0.5, so the cuts would shrink GDP only modestly.",
    "In the event, multipliers were much larger, so GDP fell further than predicted.",
    "A smaller GDP mechanically lowers the debt ratio, so austerity worked as intended.",
    "The lesson is that consolidation in a slump can be self-defeating."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "A smaller GDP raises the debt ratio, not lowers it — that is why the consolidation was self-defeating.",
  "why": "The denominator shrank, so the ratio the policy meant to cut rose."
};

const fpRicardoCloze = {
  "text": "Ricardian equivalence says today's deficit is tomorrow's %%0%%, so forward-looking households %%1%% the stimulus rather than spend it; empirically the effect is %%2%% because many households are liquidity-constrained.",
  "answers": [
    [
      "tax",
      "taxes"
    ],
    [
      "save"
    ],
    [
      "partial"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "If the deficit is just deferred taxation, a perfectly forward-looking household saves the windfall."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/intermediate/fiscal-policy-public-debt">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Economics lesson. It assumes the two foundational lessons of this
    subject: supply and demand (markets, and the circular flow of spending and income) and money
    and inflation (what money is, who creates it, and why it loses value — inflation returns
    below as the escape valve in the currency question). The intermediate lessons on GDP and
    unemployment pair naturally with this one, but nothing here requires them.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>name the three fiscal levers, and explain what the automatic stabilisers do with no new legislation;</li>
    <li>compute the Keynesian multiplier from the marginal propensity to consume, and say why real multipliers are smaller;</li>
    <li>read the debt-dynamics inequality: when the debt ratio drifts down, and when it spirals;</li>
    <li>unpack "we owe it to ourselves" into the costs that survive the accounting;</li>
    <li>explain why borrowing in your own currency changes the crisis menu, and recognise the three signatures of a debt crisis.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Fiscal policy is <strong>government spending and taxation as an economic lever</strong>. In
    recessions Keynes argued for deficits (spend now, repay later); the opposite advice —
    austerity — says debts smother growth. Both are right, in different conditions. The deciding
    arithmetic: <strong>debt stabilises when GDP growth exceeds the interest rate</strong>, and
    spirals when it doesn't. That single sentence contains the whole modern debate: it explains
    why post-war governments grew their way out of far larger debts than today's, why the
    low-interest 2010s made borrowing cheap and the rate-rises of the 2020s made it suddenly
    expensive, and why every austerity programme is ultimately an argument about that
    differential. This page covers the fiscal toolkit (spending, taxes, transfers, automatic
    stabilisers), the Keynesian multiplier with its arithmetic, the debt-dynamics equation the
    widget computes, who really bears the debt, and the live debates — multipliers, crowding
    out, Ricardian equivalence and the currency question — that decide when deficits are
    medicine and when they are the disease.
  </p>
  <Widget no="1" caption="automatic or discretionary?" widget="sort-buckets" config={fpStabiliserSort} />

  <Widget no="2" caption="the debt dynamics equation — drag growth, interest and debt and watch which side of the spiral you're on" widget="sliderLab" config={fiscalCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> drag the growth rate above the interest rate and watch the
    trajectory readout flip sign — with growth doing the stabilising, the debt ratio shrinks even
    with no surplus at all. Then drag growth below the interest rate and watch the third readout
    start demanding a primary surplus measured in real money, bigger the higher the debt ratio
    sits. The same debt feels like nothing or like a crisis depending entirely on that one
    differential.
  </p>

  <p>
    With growth above the interest rate, a country can run small deficits forever while debt
    shrinks relative to income (the post-war US and UK grew their way down from 120%+ debts).
    When interest exceeds growth, stabilisation demands primary surpluses — the arithmetic behind
    every austerity programme, and the reason the low-interest 2010s were kinder to borrowers
    than the 2020s.
  </p>

  <h2>The toolkit: spending, taxes and the built-in shock absorbers</h2>
  <p>
    Fiscal policy moves three levers. <strong>Government purchases (G)</strong> buy output
    directly — infrastructure, defence, health services. <strong>Transfer payments</strong>
    (pensions, benefits, subsidies) redistribute income without buying output —
    they stimulate only when recipients spend them. <strong>Taxes</strong> withdraw
    purchasing power; cutting them is a stimulus, raising them a brake. Each
    lever has a different speed and multiplier, which is the practical art of
    fiscal design. Alongside the discretionary choices sits the
    <strong>automatic stabilisers</strong>: in a downturn, tax receipts fall
    (incomes fell) and unemployment benefits rise (the rules say so) — the
    deficit widens with <em>no decision at all</em>, cushioning the fall in
    private spending. In a boom the same machinery runs in reverse. This is
    why recessions produce deficits under any government and any doctrine,
    and why comparing budgets across years requires separating the
    <em>cyclical</em> deficit (the stabilisers' work) from the
    <em>structural</em> deficit (the underlying policy stance) — a
    distinction the fiscal lessons of every finance ministry depend on.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each lever with its example" widget="match" config={fpLeversMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a budget deficit is always evidence of overspending. The cyclical-versus-structural distinction exists precisely because it is not: in any recession, tax receipts fall and benefit payments rise with no new legislation, so the deficit widens under any government and any doctrine. Reading a slump-year deficit as a chosen policy stance is reading the stabilisers' automatic work as if someone had decided it.</p>

  <h2>The Keynesian multiplier: why £1 of spending can move more than £1</h2>
  <p>
    One person's spending is another person's income, so a fiscal injection
    circulates. <strong>The arithmetic:</strong> if households spend 80% of any
    extra income (the marginal propensity to consume, MPC = 0.8), a £10bn
    infrastructure programme pays £10bn of income, of which £8bn is
    re-spent, becoming £8bn of income, of which £6.4bn is re-spent… The
    geometric series sums to £10bn × 1/(1 − 0.8) = <strong>£50bn</strong> — the
    <strong>multiplier</strong> is 1/(1 − MPC). <strong>Worked against reality:</strong>
    the frictionless 5 overstates it, because spending leaks at every
    round into taxes, imports and saving (a smaller "effective" MPC);
    empirical multipliers run near 0.5–1.5 for tax cuts and ordinary
    spending, and higher — 1.5 to 2+ — for government purchases in deep
    slumps with idle resources and pinned interest rates (the 2009 and
    2020 stimulus packages were designed on exactly this logic). The
    flip side is the <strong>crowding-out</strong> concern: near full
    employment, deficit borrowing competes for scarce resources,
    pushing up interest rates and squeezing private investment — the
    multiplier shrinks toward zero and can go negative in an
    overheating economy. Timing, not just size, is the policy variable:
    the same programme is medicine in a slump and congestion in a boom.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="call the multiplier" widget="estimate" config={fpMultiplierEst} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> government spending merely moves money around, so stimulus cannot raise total output. The multiplier is the refutation: one person's spending is another person's income, and in a slump with idle workers and factories that chain turns underused resources into production — the money moves, and the moving is the mechanism by which real output rises. At full capacity the same argument collapses into crowding out.</p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> the fiscal levers spend, transfer and tax, with the stabilisers running underneath, and the multiplier decides how hard a given injection lands. Next comes the arithmetic that decides whether the resulting debt is a gentle slope or a spiral.</p>

  <h2>The debt-dynamics equation: r versus g</h2>
  <p>
    The debt ratio D (debt ÷ GDP) evolves by one inequality. With
    <strong>i</strong> the average interest rate on the debt and <strong>y</strong> nominal
    GDP growth, the ratio drifts up when i exceeds y and drifts down when
    y exceeds i — modified by the <strong>primary balance</strong> (the budget
    balance excluding interest payments). <strong>Worked examples.</strong>
    <em>Post-war Britain:</em> debt over 250% of GDP in 1946; with
    decades of growth above bond yields and moderate primary balances,
    the ratio fell below 50% by the 1970s — no austerity large enough
    to explain it; growth did the work. <em>The 2010s:</em> interest
    rates fell below growth across advanced economies (the r − g
    differential turned sharply negative), making debt cheap to carry —
    the empirical backdrop of Olivier Blanchard's 2019 presidential
    address, "Public Debt and Low Interest Rates", which argued that
    when r is below g, debt may carry little fiscal cost.
    <em>The 2020s:</em> the differential swung positive as rates rose,
    and the widget's third readout — the primary surplus needed to
    stabilise, (i − y) × D — started demanding real money: at
    i = 5%, y = 3%, D = 100%, that is a 2% of GDP primary surplus,
    roughly the size of a country's entire defence budget. Debt is
    not a cliff; it is a <em>slope</em>, set by that differential —
    and the whole politics of fiscal rules (the EU's 3% and 60%
    benchmarks, the UK's fiscal mandates) is an argument about
    where on the slope to stand.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="mark the stabilising primary surplus" widget="place" config={fpDebtPlace} />

  <h2>Who bears the debt?</h2>
  <p>
    "The debt falls on our grandchildren" needs unpacking. If the bonds are
    held domestically, the future taxpayers who service the debt largely
    pay the future bondholders who receive it — "we owe it to ourselves",
    and the transfer is distributional, not international. But three real
    costs survive. <strong>Incentive costs:</strong> servicing debt requires taxes
    that distort work and investment. <strong>Foreign-held debt:</strong> the
    share of US Treasuries held abroad (roughly a third) and of UK gilts
    (roughly a quarter) is a genuine external claim. <strong>Reduced policy
    space:</strong> a country that arrives at the next crisis already deeply
    indebted has less room to respond — the 2010 eurozone members, pre-
    constrained when the shock came, are the case study. And the
    distribution within the future matters: bond interest flows to the
    savers (wealthier, on average), financed by taxpayers (broader) —
    a regressive pump running quietly through the years.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="true or false — who bears the debt" widget="judge" config={fpBurdenJudge} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> if the debt is owed "to ourselves", it costs nothing. Domestic ownership makes the servicing a distributional transfer rather than an international loss, but the incentive costs of the taxes that service it, the foreign-held share of Treasuries and gilts, and the crisis-response room a heavily indebted country has already spent all survive the accounting. "We owe it to ourselves" is where the analysis starts, not where it ends.</p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> domestic ownership makes debt servicing a distributional transfer, but incentive costs, the foreign-held share and lost crisis room all survive the accounting. One variable changes the risk picture more than any other — the currency the debt is denominated in.</p>

  <h2>The currency question: Japan, Greece and the escape valve</h2>
  <p>
    The single biggest risk factor in public debt is the currency it is
    denominated in. A government borrowing in <strong>its own currency that it
    issues</strong> (US, UK, Japan — Japan's debt exceeds 250% of GDP with
    borrowing costs near zero) cannot be forced into involuntary default:
    the central bank can always create the currency, and the crisis mode
    is <em>inflation</em>, not bankruptcy. A government borrowing in
    <strong>foreign currency</strong> (many developing nations in dollars) or
    sharing a <strong>currency it does not print</strong> (Greece within the
    eurozone, 2010–2015) faces genuine default risk: it cannot conjure
    dollars or euros, so when markets doubt repayment, yields spike,
    refinancing closes, and the IMF arrives with conditions. This
    distinction explains otherwise puzzling patterns: why Japan's
    enormous debt is calm while Greece's much smaller (relative) debt
    was catastrophic; why eurozone members accepted harsher fiscal
    rules than the US or UK would tolerate; and why "print versus
    default" is the real menu behind sovereign-debt crises — with
    inflation as the escape valve that prints the bill onto savers
    rather than taxpayers.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="own currency or foreign currency?" widget="branch" config={fpCurrencyBranch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a national debt is like a household
  mortgage — someday it must all be paid off." Governments do not retire;
  the debt is perpetually refinanced, and the meaningful quantity is its
  <em>ratio to income</em>, managed through growth and inflation as much as
  through surpluses. No national debt has ever been "paid off" in the
  household sense — the UK has carried a national debt continuously since
  the 1690s. The household metaphor fails precisely where the r-versus-g
  arithmetic lives.</p>

  <h2>Three signatures of a debt crisis</h2>
  <p>
    Because the slope is gradual, crises announce themselves with recognisable
    patterns. <strong>Signature one — the rollover squeeze:</strong> a government
    must refinance maturing bonds continuously; when markets doubt it, new
    issues demand punitive yields, the interest bill eats the budget, and
    the spiral the widget models turns self-fulfilling (Greece, 2010–12:
    two-year yields passed 100% at the peak). <strong>Signature two — the
    currency run:</strong> foreign-currency borrowers face the wall sooner,
    because the exchange rate collapses first and the debt (in local
    terms) balloons behind it — Argentina's repeated crises, Sri Lanka's
    2022 default. <strong>Signature three — the inflation escape:</strong>
    own-currency borrowers avoid formal default by letting the real
    value of the debt erode — debt holders paid in devalued currency —
    the "financial repression" mix of capped rates and inflation that
    worked through the post-war decades. Each signature sets the
    policy menu that follows: IMF programmes and haircuts for the
    first, debt restructurings for the second, and for the third a
    bond market that demands inflation-indexed instruments — which
    is why so much modern debt now <em>is</em> indexed: creditors
    legislated themselves out of the escape valve.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="how a rollover crisis unfolds" widget="order" config={fpCrisisOrder} />

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> crises announce themselves with recognisable patterns — the rollover squeeze, the currency run, the inflation escape. Governments that can see the slope coming have repeatedly tried to legislate discipline onto their future selves.</p>

  <h2>Fiscal rules: trying to legislate discipline</h2>
  <p>
    Because deficits are politically easy and consolidation politically
    hard, governments have repeatedly tried to bind their future selves
    with <strong>fiscal rules</strong> — and the attempts teach as much as the
    failures. The EU's Maastricht criteria (deficits under 3% of GDP,
    debt under 60%) were breached by France and Germany themselves
    within years; the UK has rewritten its fiscal mandate with nearly
    every government since 1997; Switzerland's "debt brake" and
    Chile's structural-balance rule are the cited successes —
    constitutional-grade, cyclical-adjusted, and enforced by
    referendum or independent council. The design lessons are
    consistent: rules must be <strong>cyclically adjusted</strong> (so
    recessions do not force self-defeating cuts — the eurozone's
    2010 lesson), <strong>leave room for investment</strong> (the
    "golden rule" idea: borrow for assets, not for
    consumption), and <strong>carry an escape clause</strong> for
    genuine emergencies (the pandemic suspended every rule in
    Europe simultaneously). The unresolved objection is
    political economy: a rule that binds is unpopular, a
    rule that does not bind is decoration — and every
    generation rediscovers the trade-off.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="headline versus structural deficit — drag the divider" widget="compare" config={fpDeficitCompare} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A government spends 10 billion on infrastructure in a slump, and households spend 80% of any extra income. Roughly how large is the total rise in GDP in the frictionless case — and why is the real figure smaller?</li>
    <li>Debt is 100% of GDP, the average interest rate is 3%, and nominal GDP grows 5%. What keeps the debt ratio in check?</li>
    <li>Why does borrowing in your own currency make a debt crisis less likely — and what is the cost of that safety?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The multiplier is 1/(1 - 0.8) = 5, so about 50 billion in the frictionless chain — but spending leaks into taxes, imports and saving at every round, and multipliers near full employment are far smaller than in deep slumps with idle resources. (2) Nothing needs repaying: with growth above the interest rate, the ratio drifts down even alongside a small primary deficit — growth itself does the stabilising, as post-war Britain and the US demonstrated. (3) A government issuing debt in currency it prints cannot be forced into involuntary default; the escape valve is inflation, which taxes savers rather than forcing bankruptcy — the reason Japan's enormous debt is calm while foreign-currency or shared-currency borrowers face genuine default risk.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Three levers — purchases, transfers, taxes — with the automatic stabilisers cushioning the cycle before any parliament votes.</li>
    <li>The multiplier is 1/(1 - MPC) in the frictionless case; leakages shrink it, idle resources enlarge it, and timing decides whether stimulus is medicine or congestion.</li>
    <li>The debt ratio drifts down when growth exceeds the interest rate and up when it does not, modified by the primary balance.</li>
    <li>"We owe it to ourselves" still leaves incentive costs, the foreign-held share and lost crisis room.</li>
    <li>Own-currency debt cannot force default — the escape valve is inflation; foreign-currency and shared-currency debt can.</li>
    <li>Crises announce themselves: the rollover squeeze, the currency run, the inflation escape.</li>
    <li>Fiscal rules must be cyclically adjusted, leave room for investment, and carry an escape clause — or they bind too hard or not at all.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The natural next lesson is monetary-policy-central-banks: the interest rate in the widget's
    readouts is largely the central bank's choice, and the fiscal and monetary levers act on the
    same economy at the same time. unemployment-labour-markets shows the slack that decides the
    multiplier's size, and gdp-national-accounts shows where the GDP in every ratio here comes
    from.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The austerity debate, in one paragraph.</strong> After 2010, eurozone
    austerity (Alesina and Ardagna's "expansionary austerity" was the
    intellectual cover) met the IMF's own mea culpa: multipliers in the
    slump were far larger than assumed, so the Greek GDP collapse was
    deeper and longer than the models predicted. The synthesis: fiscal
    consolidation is eventually necessary where r exceeds g durably, but
    its timing and speed matter enormously — consolidation in a slump
    with a negative differential is self-defeating (smaller GDP raises
    the debt ratio it was meant to cut).
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="find the self-defeating arithmetic" widget="spot-error" config={fpAusteritySpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>Ricardian equivalence and its limits.</strong> Robert Barro's argument:
    if households are perfectly forward-looking, today's deficit is
    tomorrow's taxes, so they save the stimulus rather than spend it —
    the multiplier is zero. Empirically partial: households are
    liquidity-constrained, myopic and uncertain, so they do spend —
    but the argument disciplines claims about painless deficits.
  </p>
  <Widget no="11" caption="fill the blanks — Ricardian equivalence" widget="cloze" config={fpRicardoCloze} />
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> How high can the r − g differential rise
    before debt dynamics truly bind (the 2020s rate regime is the live
    test)? Should fiscal rules target debt, deficits or investment
    (the "golden rule" debate)? And does the political economy of
    deficits — easy to raise, hard to reverse — mean discipline must
    be legislated, or do rules merely shift the timing of the
    inevitable argument? The General Theory is ninety years old and
    the argument is exactly where Keynes left it, with better data.
  </p>
